#import "template.typ": front-matter-page, stamp-header, redacted

#front-matter-page(page-count: 24)[
  #stamp-header("Intake Protocol")
  #v(0.6em)
  #line(length: 100%, stroke: 0.4pt)
  #v(0.8em)
  #align(center, text(size: 22pt, weight: "bold", tracking: 1pt)[HOW TO OPEN A CASE FILE])
  #v(1.4em)

  Every chapter in this dossier is a *Case File*: five linked dilemmas,
  one theme, one moment where the file is opened. Nothing in it changes
  depending on how you open it - the same five dilemmas are waiting either
  way. What changes is who is in the room with you.

  #v(1em)
  #line(length: 100%, stroke: 0.3pt)
  #v(1em)

  #text(font: "DejaVu Sans Mono", size: 9pt, weight: "bold", tracking: 1pt)[SOLO VERDICT]
  #v(0.4em)
  Scan the left code. You will answer the five dilemmas on your own, at
  your own pace, and receive your verdict privately when you finish. Read
  each Exhibit printed below before or after you answer in the app - the
  book carries the full case either way.

  #v(1em)

  #text(font: "DejaVu Sans Mono", size: 9pt, weight: "bold", tracking: 1pt)[CONVENE TRIBUNAL]
  #v(0.4em)
  Scan the right code instead when there is more than one of you at the
  table. This opens a shared room for that specific Case File. Whoever
  opens it becomes the Tribunal's narrator: read each Exhibit aloud from
  the page while everyone else has the room open on their own phone.
  Nobody needs to read ahead - the narrator sets the pace by turning the
  page, which is also the cue to move to the next Exhibit in the room.
  Each of you answers privately on your own screen; the verdict is
  revealed to the whole table once everyone has answered.

  #v(1em)
  #line(length: 100%, stroke: 0.3pt)
  #v(1em)

  A code only ever opens *its own* Case File - the five dilemmas printed
  under it, never a different five. #redacted(width: 3em) rooms cannot be
  reopened once a Tribunal has delivered its verdict; scan the code again
  for a fresh case elsewhere in the file.
]
